<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ye Ladie Greene Sleeves answere to Donkyn hir frende (Edward White)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1580</span><span class="sg-chain-h">answer song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A New Courtly Sonnet of the Lady Green Sleeves (A Handful of Pleasant Delights)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1584</span><span class="sg-chain-h">printed version, &quot;to the new tune of Green Sleeves&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Merry Wives of Windsor (William Shakespeare)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1597</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (the tune named)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Old England Grown New (also called The Blacksmith)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">17th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Christmas and New Year texts</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">from 1686</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Green Sleeves to a Ground, in The Division Flute</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1706</span><span class="sg-chain-h">15 divisions on the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">What Child Is This? (William Chatterton Dix)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1865</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Second Suite in F for Military Band; St Paul's Suite (Gustav Holst)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">tune woven in with &quot;Dargason&quot;</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sir John in Love (Ralph Vaughan Williams)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1928</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as the song for Mistress Ford in Act III</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fantasia on Greensleeves (arranged by Ralph Greaves)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1934</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arranged for strings and harp</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Towers in the Mist (Elizabeth Goudge)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1938</span><span class="sg-chain-h">last verse and chorus quoted in a novel</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Home in the Meadow (How the West Was Won)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1962</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li></ul></section>
